course: dictionary
lesson: 3522
title: Favourite and its many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Favourite is a word you use every day. It means liked the most, the one you like best of all. In «My favourite colour is green», the word my and the word colour tell you that green is the colour you like more than any other.
explain: Favourite can also be a noun, meaning the thing or person that is liked best. In «Children often feel a parent has a favourite», the context shows that a favourite is a person chosen above the rest.
explain: In a race or contest, the favourite is the one expected to win. Read the words around it to choose the right meaning. Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Favourite is an adjective when it describes a noun, as in your favourite food.
fact: Favourite is a noun when it names the thing or person itself, as in a favourite or favourites.
fact: «Favourite» means liked the most.
fact: In a contest, the favourite is the one expected to win.
fact: Britain keeps the u in «favourite»; American spelling writes «favorite», and both spellings are correct in their own country.
exercise: «My favourite food is pasta.» What does favourite mean here? | *Liked the most | Hated the most | Very cheap | Cooked the longest
exercise: «Everyone bet on the favourite to win the race.» What does favourite mean here? | The last one to finish | The slowest runner | *The one expected to win | The horse that got lost
exercise: «Poets paint pictures with words, and they have a few favourite tricks.» What kind of word is favourite here? | A verb showing an action | *An adjective, describing the tricks | A number | A noun naming a person
test: «Children often feel a parent has a favourite.» What is a favourite in this sentence? | A type of school | A birthday present | *The person a parent likes best | A toy that is broken
test: What is the best way to check the meaning of a new word? | Ignore the sentence and guess the longest word | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Stop reading and never try again | Look only at the first letter
exam: Ella says, "To play favourites means to play nicely with everyone." Is she right? | Yes, it means playing games together | *No, it means treating one person better than the others | Yes, it means being fair to all | No, it means playing outside
